==English==

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|inter-|breed}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪnteɹˈbɹid/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-interbreed.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːd|s=3}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|||interbred}}

# To [[breed]] or [[reproduce]] within an isolated [[community]].
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[breed]] or [[reproduce]] within a [[heterogenous]] [[community]], the products of which produce [[hybrid]]s.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1909|title=Knowledge and Illustrated Scientific News|volume=32|page=426
|passage=The rypes and our red grouse are identically the same bird, though disease has never been known in the former, and even '''interbreeding''' with the red grouse, to the limited extent they are ever likely to do, should do more good than harm.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=John Tierney|title=The Big City; Stray Dogs As a Litigant's Best Friend|magazine=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2001/04/13/nyregion/the-big-city-stray-dogs-as-a-litigant-s-best-friend.html|date=13-04-2001|passage=The city's two-inch Environmental Review Technical Manual instructs regulators to consider adverse environmental impacts on "any subspecies" or "any distinct population segment . . . which '''interbreeds''' when mature."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2009 January 29|author=Ray Collier|title=Country diary|journal=w:The Guardian|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/environment/2009/jan/29/country-diary-highlands-collier-rural
|passage=The rock dove has so '''interbred''' with the feral pigeons from doocots and racing pigeon lofts that, as a pure species, it may possibly now be extinct in Britain.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Carl Zimmer|title=Why Are Some Mice (and People) Monogamous? A Study Points to Genes|magazine=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/04/19/science/parenting-genes-study.html|date=19-04-2017|passage=In the wild, deer mice and oldfield mice never '''interbreed'''.}}

